@startuml

title ORE as an authorization server: the client asks, the person consents, IAM grants

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
autonumber

actor "the person" as person
participant "the client\n(an agent, or a\nperson's tool)" as client
participant "ores.http\n(authorization server)" as gateway
participant "ores.iam" as iam
participant "the API\n(ores.http)" as api

client -> gateway : GET /authorize, with client id, redirect, scope, state, PKCE
gateway --> person : the sign-in, then the consent screen
person -> gateway : approve the grant
gateway -> iam : create the grant this consent names
note right
  The grant an approval creates is the grant
  an agent key creates: one entity, so the
  audit and the revocation are the same act
  whichever door the client came through.
end note
iam --> gateway : the grant, and its code
gateway --> client : 302 to the client's address, with code and state
client -> gateway : POST /token, with the code and the PKCE verifier
gateway --> client : a token whose subject is the person and whose actor is the client
client -> api : the call, bearing the token
api -> iam : the keys that verify it
api -> api : authorise what the grant allows, and nothing more

@enduml
